==Types==
There are two classes of NTDs: ''open'', which are more common, and ''closed''. Open NTDs occur when the brain and/or [[spinal cord]] are exposed at birth through a defect in the skull or [[vertebra]]e (spinal column). Open NTDs include [[anencephaly]], [[encephalocele]]s, [[hydranencephaly]], [[iniencephaly]], [[schizencephaly]], and the most common form, [[spina bifida]]. Closed NTDs occur when the spinal defect is covered by skin. Types of closed NTDs include lipomeningocele, lipomyelomeningocele, and [[Tethered spinal cord syndrome|tethered cord]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Avagliano |first1=Laura |last2=Massa |first2=Valentina |last3=George |first3=Timothy M. |last4=Qureshy |first4=Sarah |last5=Bulfamante |first5=Gaetano |last6=Finnell |first6=Richard H. |date=2019-11-15 |title=Overview on Neural tube defects: from development to physical characteristics |journal=Birth Defects Research |volume=111 |issue=19 |pages=1455–1467 |doi=10.1002/bdr2.1380 |issn=2472-1727 |pmc=6511489 |pmid=30421543}}</ref>

=== Anencephaly ===
[[Anencephaly]] (without brain) is a severe neural tube defect that occurs when the anterior-most end of the neural tube fails to close, usually during the 23rd and 26th days of pregnancy. This results in an absence of a major portion of the brain and skull. Infants born with this condition lack the main part of the forebrain and are usually blind, deaf and display major craniofacial anomalies. The lack of a functioning [[cerebrum]] will prevent the infant from even gaining consciousness. Infants are either stillborn or usually die within a few hours or days after birth.<ref>{{Cite web |last=CDC |date=2019-12-04 |title=Facts about Craniosynostosis {{!}} CDC |url=https://www.cdc.gov/ncbddd/birthdefects/craniosynostosis.html |access-date=2022-11-28 |website=Centers for Disease Control and Prevention |language=en-us}}</ref> For example, [[anencephaly]] in humans can result from mutations in the [[NUAK2]] kinase.<ref name="Bonnard">{{Cite journal|last1=Bonnard|first1=Carine|last2=Navaratnam|first2=Naveenan|last3=Ghosh|first3=Kakaly|last4=Chan|first4=Puck Wee|last5=Tan|first5=Thong Teck|last6=Pomp|first6=Oz|last7=Ng|first7=Alvin Yu Jin|last8=Tohari|first8=Sumanty|last9=Changede|first9=Rishita|last10=Carling|first10=David|last11=Venkatesh|first11=Byrappa|date=2020-12-07|title=A loss-of-function NUAK2 mutation in humans causes anencephaly due to impaired Hippo-YAP signaling|journal=The Journal of Experimental Medicine|volume=217|issue=12|article-number=e20191561 |doi=10.1084/jem.20191561|issn=1540-9538|pmc=7953732|pmid=32845958}}</ref>

===Encephaloceles===
[[Encephalocele]]s are characterized by protrusions of the brain through the skull that are sac-like and covered with membrane. They can be a groove down the middle of the upper part of the skull, between the forehead and nose, or the back of the skull.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Encephaloceles {{!}} National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke |url=https://www.ninds.nih.gov/health-information/disorders/birth-disorders-brain-and-spinal-cord#encephalocele |access-date=2022-11-28 |website=www.ninds.nih.gov}}</ref> Due to the range in its location, encephaloceles are classified by the location as well as the type of defect it causes. Subtypes include occipital encephalocele, [[encephalocele]] of the cranial vault, and nasal encephaloceles (frontoethmoidal encephaloceles and basal encephaloceles), with approximately 80% of all encephaloceles occurring in the occipital area.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Abdel-Aziz |first1=Mosaad |last2=El-Bosraty |first2=Hussam |date=2010 |title="Nasal encephalocele: Endoscopic excision with anesthetic consideration" |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0165587610002065#! |journal=International Journal of Pediatric Otorhinolaryngology |volume=74 |issue=8 |pages=869–873 |doi=10.1016/j.ijporl.2010.04.015 |pmid=20554034 |access-date=2020-11-19|url-access=subscription }}</ref> Encephaloceles are often obvious and diagnosed immediately. Sometimes small encephaloceles in the nasal and forehead are undetected.<ref name="NINDS">{{cite web |url=https://www.ninds.nih.gov/Disorders/All-Disorders/Encephaloceles-Information-Page |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170104203501/http://www.ninds.nih.gov/Disorders/All-Disorders/Encephaloceles-Information-Page |archive-date=January 4, 2017 |title=Encephaloceles Information Page |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=NIH |publisher=National institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke |access-date=September 15, 2017 }}</ref> Despite the wide range in its implications, encephaloceles are most likely to be caused by improper separation of the surface ectoderm and the [[neuroectoderm]] after the closure of the neural folds in the fourth week of gastrulation.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Broekman |first1=Marike |last2=Hoving |first2=Eelco |date=2008 |title="Nasal encephalocele in a child with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome" |journal=Journal of Neurosurgery |volume=6 |issue=1 |pages=485–7 |doi=10.3171/PED/2008/1/6/485 |pmid=18518702 }}</ref>

===Hydranencephaly===
[[Hydranencephaly]] is a condition in which the [[cerebral hemispheres]] are missing and instead filled with sacs of cerebrospinal fluid. People are born with hydranencephaly, but most of the time, the symptoms appear in a later stage. Newborns with hydrancephaly can swallow, cry, sleep and their head is in proportion to their body.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Pavone |first1=Piero |last2=Praticò |first2=Andrea D |last3=Vitaliti |first3=Giovanna |last4=Ruggieri |first4=Martino |last5=Rizzo |first5=Renata |last6=Parano |first6=Enrico |last7=Pavone |first7=Lorenzo |last8=Pero |first8=Giuseppe |last9=Falsaperla |first9=Raffaele |date=2014-10-18 |title=Hydranencephaly: cerebral spinal fluid instead of cerebral mantles |journal=Italian Journal of Pediatrics |volume=40 |article-number=79 |doi=10.1186/s13052-014-0079-1 |issn=1824-7288 |pmc=4421920 |pmid=25326191 |doi-access=free }}</ref> However, after a few weeks, the infants develop increased [[muscle tone]] and irritability. After a few months, the brain start to fill with cerebrospinal fluid (hydrocephalus). This has several consequences. Infants start to develop problems with seeing, hearing, growing, and learning. The missing parts of the brain and the amount of cerebrospinal fluid can also lead to seizures, spasm, problems with regulating their body temperature, and breathing and digestion problems. Besides problems in the brain, hydranencephaly can also be seen on the outside of the body. Hydrocephalus leads to more [[cerebrospinal fluid]] in the brain, which can result in an enlarged head.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Hydranencephaly Information Page|url=https://www.ninds.nih.gov/health-information/disorders/birth-disorders-brain-and-spinal-cord|date=2019-03-27|website=National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Hydranencephaly|url=https://rarediseases.org/rare-diseases/hydranencephaly/|website=NORD (National Organization for Rare Disorders)|language=en-US|access-date=2020-05-12}}</ref>

The cause of hydranencephaly is not clear. Hydranencephaly is a result of an injury of the nervous system or an abnormal development of the nervous system. The neural tube closes in the sixth week of the pregnancy,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Fetal development: What happens during the first trimester?|url=https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/pregnancy-week-by-week/in-depth/prenatal-care/art-20045302|website=Mayo Clinic|language=en|access-date=2020-05-12}}</ref> so hydranencephaly develops during these weeks of the pregnancy. The cause of these injuries/development is not clear.<ref>{{Citation |last1=Sandoval |first1=Jose I. |title=Hydranencephaly |date=2022 |url=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558991/ |work=StatPearls |place=Treasure Island (FL) |publisher=StatPearls Publishing |pmid=32644417 |access-date=2022-11-28 |last2=De Jesus |first2=Orlando}}</ref>

Theories regarding the causes of hydrancephaly include:
* blockage in the carotid artery: some researchers think that a blockage of the carotid artery leads to the under-/no development of the brain.{{medical citation needed|date=July 2023}}
* inherited condition.
* infections: during the pregnancy, a woman can develop an infection in the uterus what can lead to problems with the neural tube.{{medical citation needed|date=July 2023}}
* environmental toxins: during the pregnancy, a woman can be exposed to environmental toxins that may have effect on the health of the infant.{{medical citation needed|date=July 2023}}

===Iniencephaly===
[[Iniencephaly]] is a rare neural tube defect that results in extreme bending of the head to the spine. The diagnosis can usually be made on antenatal ultrasound scanning, but if not will undoubtedly be made immediately after birth because the head is bent backwards and the face looks upwards. Usually the neck is absent. The skin of the face connects directly to the chest and the scalp connects to the upper back. Individuals with iniencephaly generally die within a few hours after birth.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Korostyshevskaya |first1=Alexandra |last2=Gornostaeva |first2=Alyona |last3=Volkov |first3=Rem |last4=Yarnykh |first4=Vasily |date=2020-11-19 |title=Iniencephaly: radiologic and pathomorphologic perinatal observation |journal=Radiology Case Reports |volume=16 |issue=1 |pages=201–204 |doi=10.1016/j.radcr.2020.11.003 |issn=1930-0433 |pmc=7689276 |pmid=33294089}}</ref>

===Spina bifida===
[[Spina bifida]] is further divided into two subclasses, spina bifida cystica and spina bifida occulta.<ref>{{Citation |last1=Brea |first1=Cristina M. |title=Spina Bifida |date=2022 |url=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559265/ |work=StatPearls |place=Treasure Island (FL) |publisher=StatPearls Publishing |pmid=32644691 |access-date=2022-11-28 |last2=Munakomi |first2=Sunil}}</ref>
* Spina bifida cystica includes [[meningocele]] and [[myelomeningocele]]. Meningocele is less severe and is characterized by herniation of the [[meninges]], but not the spinal cord, through the opening in the [[spinal canal]]. Myelomeningocele involves herniation of the meninges as well as the spinal cord through the opening.<ref name="firstaid">
{{Cite book | last1 = Le | first1 = Tao | first2 = Vikas | last2 = Bhushan | first3 = Neil | last3 = Vasan | title = First Aid for the USMLE Step 1: 2010 | edition = 20th | publisher = McGraw-Hill | year = 2010 | page = [https://archive.org/details/firstaidforusmle0000unse_r6p4/page/127 127] | isbn = 978-0-07-163340-6 | url = https://archive.org/details/firstaidforusmle0000unse_r6p4/page/127 }}
</ref>
* Spina bifida occulta means ''hidden split spine''.<ref name="saladin">{{Cite book   | last1 = Saladin | first1 = Kenneth  | title = Anatomy and Physiology: The Unity of Form and Function  | url = https://archive.org/details/anatomyphysiolog00sala_868 | url-access = limited | publisher = McGraw-Hill  | year= 2010  | page = [https://archive.org/details/anatomyphysiolog00sala_868/page/n505 485]  | isbn = 978-0-07-352569-3 }}</ref> In this type of neural tube defect, the meninges do not herniate through the opening in the spinal canal.<ref name="firstaid" /> The most frequently seen form of spina bifida occulta is when parts of the bones of the spine, called the [[spinous process]], and the [[neural arch]] appear abnormal on a radiogram, without involvement of the spinal cord and spinal nerves.<ref>{{cite journal | last1 = Pittman | first1 = T | title = Spina bifida occulta. | journal = Journal of Neurosurgery. Pediatrics | volume = 1 | issue = 2 | page = 113 | year = 2008 | pmid = 18352777 | doi = 10.3171/PED/2008/1/2/113 | doi-access =  }}</ref> The risk of recurrence in those who have a first degree relative (a parent or sibling) is 5–10 times greater compared to the general population.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Spina Bifida Occulta (for Parents) - Nemours KidsHealth |url=https://kidshealth.org/en/parents/spina-bifida-occulta.html |access-date=2022-11-28 |website=kidshealth.org}}</ref>

==Causes==

===Folate deficiency===
Inadequate levels of [[folate]] (vitamin B<sub>9</sub>) and [[vitamin B12|vitamin B<sub>12</sub>]] during pregnancy have been found to lead to increased risk of NTDs.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2">{{Cite journal|last1=De-Regil|first1=Luz Maria|last2=Peña-Rosas|first2=Juan Pablo|last3=Fernández-Gaxiola|first3=Ana C.|last4=Rayco-Solon|first4=Pura|date=2015-12-14|title=Effects and safety of periconceptional oral folate supplementation for preventing birth defects|journal=The Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews|volume=2015|issue=12|article-number=CD007950|doi=10.1002/14651858.CD007950.pub3|issn=1469-493X|pmid=26662928|pmc=8783750}}</ref> Although both are part of the same biopathway, [[folate deficiency]] is much more common and therefore more of a concern.<ref name=":1">{{cite journal | doi = 10.1542/peds.2008-1173 | last1 = Molloy | first1 = A. M. | last2 = Kirke | first2 = P. N. | last3 = Troendle | first3 = J. F. | last4 = Burke | first4 = H. | last5 = Sutton | first5 = M. | last6 = Brody | first6 = L. C. | last7 = Scott | year = 2009 | first7 = JM | last8 = Mills | first8 = JL | title = Maternal Vitamin B-12 Status and Risk of Neural Tube Defects in a Population With High Neural Tube Defect Prevalence and No Folic Acid Fortification | journal = Pediatrics | volume = 123 | issue = 3| pages = 917–23 | pmid = 19255021 | pmc = 4161975 }}</ref><ref name=":2" /> Folate is required for the production and maintenance of new cells, for DNA synthesis and RNA synthesis. Folate is needed to carry one carbon groups for [[methylation]] and [[nucleic acid]] synthesis. It has been hypothesized that the early human embryo may be particularly vulnerable to folate deficiency due to differences of the functional enzymes in this pathway during embryogenesis combined with high demand for post translational methylations of the cytoskeleton in neural cells during neural tube closure.<ref>{{cite journal | author = Bjorklund N, Gordon R | year = 2006 | title = A hypothesis linking low folate intake to neural tube defects due to failure of post-translation methylations of the cytoskeleton | url = http://www.ijdb.ehu.es/web/paper.php?doi=052102nb | journal = Int. J. Dev. Biol. | volume = 50 | issue = 2–3| pages = 135–41 | doi = 10.1387/ijdb.052102nb | pmid = 16479482 | doi-access = free | url-access = subscription }}</ref> Failure of post-translational methylation of the cytoskeleton, required for differentiation has been implicated in neural tube defects.<ref>{{cite journal | author = Akchiche | year = 2012 | title = Homocysteinylation of neuronal proteins contributes to folate deficiency-associated alterations of differentiation, vesicular transport, and plasticity in hippocampal neuronal cells | journal = The FASEB Journal | volume = 26 | issue = 10| pages = 3980–92 | doi=10.1096/fj.12-205757| doi-access = free |display-authors=etal | pmid=22713523| s2cid = 30113330 }}</ref> Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> is also an important receptor in the folate biopathway  such that studies have shown deficiency in vitamin B<sub>12</sub> contributes to risk of NTDs as well.<ref>{{cite journal | doi = 10.1016/j.ymgme.2009.06.004 | last1 = Li | first1 = F. | last2 = Watkins | first2 = D. | last3 = Rosenblatt | first3 = D. S. | year = 2009 | title = Vitamin B-12 and birth defects | journal = Molecular Genetics and Metabolism | volume = 98 | issue = 1–2| pages = 166–72 | pmid = 19586788 }}</ref> There is substantial evidence that direct folic supplementation increases blood serum levels of bioavailable folate even though at least one study have shown slow and variable activity of dihydrofolate reductase in human liver.<ref>{{cite journal | author = Anderson C. | year = 2013| title = Response of serum and red blood cell folate concentrations to folic acid supplementation depends on methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase C677T genotype: results from a crossover trial | journal = Mol. Nutr. Food Res. | volume = 57 | issue = 4| pages = 637–44 | doi = 10.1002/mnfr.201200108 | pmid = 23456769|display-authors=etal| pmc = 4132693}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Bailey SW, Ayling JE | date = Sep 2009 | title = The extremely slow and variable activity of dihydrofolate reductase in human liver and its implications for high folic acid intake | journal = Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A | volume = 106 | issue = 36| pages = 15424–29 | pmc=2730961 | pmid=19706381 | doi=10.1073/pnas.0902072106 | bibcode = 2009PNAS..10615424B| doi-access = free }}</ref> A diet rich in natural folate (350&nbsp;μg/d) can show as much increase in plasma folate as taking low levels of folic acid (250&nbsp;μg/d) in individuals<ref>{{cite journal | year = 1999 | title = Dietary Folate from Vegetables and Citrus Fruit Decreases Plasma Homocysteine Concentrations in Humans in a Dietary Controlled Trial | journal = J. Nutr. | volume = 129 | issue = 6| pages = 1135–39 | doi = 10.1093/jn/129.6.1135 | pmid = 10356077 | last1 = Brouwer | first1 = I. A. | last2 = Van Dusseldorp | first2 = M. | last3 = West | first3 = C. E. | last4 = Meyboom | first4 = S. | last5 = Thomas | first5 = C. M. | last6 = Duran | first6 = M. | last7 = Van Het Hof | first7 = K. H. | last8 = Eskes | first8 = T. K. | last9 = Hautvast | first9 = J. G. | last10 = Steegers-Theunissen | first10 = R. P. | doi-access = free }}</ref> However a comparison of general population outcomes across many countries with different approaches to increasing folate consumption has found that only general food fortification with folic acid reduces neural tube defects.<ref>[http://www.bmj.com/content/330/7491/571?variant=full-text International retrospective cohort study of neural tube defects in relation to folic acid recommendations: are the recommendations working?] BMJ 2005;330:571</ref> While there have been concerns about folic acid supplementation being linked to an increased risk for cancer, a systematic review in 2012 shows there is no evidence except in the case of [[prostate cancer]] which indicates a modest increase in risk.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Wein |first1=TN |last2=Pike |first2=E |last3=Wisløff |first3=T |last4=Staff |first4=A |last5=Smeland |first5=S |last6=Klemp |first6=M |title=Cancer risk with folic acid supplements: a systematic review and meta-analysis |journal=[[BMJ Open]] |date=12 January 2012 |volume=2 |issue=1 |article-number=e000653 |doi=10.1136/bmjopen-2011-000653  |doi-access=free|pmid=22240654 |pmc=3278486}} {{open access}}</ref>

[[Choline]], closely linked to folate metabolism, is essential for fetal development and is tenfold enriched in [[amniotic fluid]] compared to maternal serum levels. Its deficiency is strongly associated with NTDs, possibly due to [[methylation|hypomethylation]] in fetal progenitor cells or impaired [[acetylcholine]] synthesis, which may regulate neuronal growth and differentiation.<ref name="Kenny2025">{{Cite journal |vauthors=Kenny TC, Scharenberg S, Abu-Remaileh M, Birsoy K |date=2025-01-08 |title=Cellular and organismal function of choline metabolism |journal=Nature Metabolism |language=en |volume=7 |issue=1 |pages=35–52 |doi=10.1038/s42255-024-01203-8 |issn=2522-5812 |pmid=39779890 |pmc=11990872 }}</ref> Studies in mice show that perinatal choline supplementation reduces neurodevelopmental abnormalities and improves memory, spatial skills, and exploratory behavior in offspring.<ref name="Kenny2025"/>

There have been studies showing the relationship between NTDs, folate deficiency and the difference of skin pigmentation within human populations across different latitudes. There are many factors that would influence the folate levels in human bodies: (i) the direct dietary intake of folic acid through fortified products, (ii) environmental agents such as UV radiation.  In concern with the latter, the UV radiation-induced folate [[photodissociation|photolysis]] has been shown via in vitro and in vivo studies to decrease the folate level and implicate in etiology of NTDs not only in humans but other amphibian species. Therefore, a protection against the UV radiation-induced photolysis of folate is imperative for the evolution of human populations living in tropical regions where the exposure to UV radiation is high over the year. One body natural adaptation is to elevate the concentration of melanin inside the skin. Melanin works as either an optical filter to disperse the incoming UV radiation rays or free radical to stabilize the hazardous photochemical products. Multiple studies have demonstrated the highly melanized integument as a defense against folate photolysis in Native Americans or African Americans correlates with lower occurrence of NTDs in general.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Jablonski|first1=Nina G.|last2=Chaplin|first2=George|date=2010-05-11|title=Human skin pigmentation as an adaptation to UV radiation|journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences|language=en|volume=107|issue=Supplement 2|pages=8962–8968|doi=10.1073/pnas.0914628107|issn=0027-8424|pmid=20445093|bibcode=2010PNAS..107.8962J|pmc=3024016|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Evolution of Human Skin Coloration — Department of Anthropology|url=https://anth.la.psu.edu/research/research-labs/jablonski-lab/research/skin-evol|website=anth.la.psu.edu|access-date=2020-05-16|archive-date=2020-08-04|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200804095734/https://anth.la.psu.edu/research/research-labs/jablonski-lab/research/skin-evol}}</ref>

=== Genetic deficiencies ===
As reported by [[Bruno Reversade]] and colleagues, the inactivation of the [[NUAK2]] kinase in humans leads to [[anencephaly]].<ref name="Bonnard"/> This fatal birth defect is believed to arise as a consequence of impaired [[Hippo signaling pathway|HIPPO]] signalling.<ref name="Bonnard"/> Other genes such as TRIM36 have also been associated with anencephaly in humans.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Singh|first1=Nivedita|last2=Kumble Bhat|first2=Vishwanath|last3=Tiwari|first3=Ankana|last4=Kodaganur|first4=Srinivas G.|last5=Tontanahal|first5=Sagar J.|last6=Sarda|first6=Astha|last7=Malini|first7=K. V.|last8=Kumar|first8=Arun|date=2017-03-15|title=A homozygous mutation in TRIM36 causes autosomal recessive anencephaly in an Indian family|journal=Human Molecular Genetics|volume=26|issue=6|pages=1104–1114|doi=10.1093/hmg/ddx020|issn=1460-2083|pmid=28087737|doi-access=free}}</ref>

===Gene-environment interaction===
A deficiency of folate itself does not cause neural tube defects. The association seen between reduced neural tube defects and folic acid supplementation is due to a [[gene-environment interaction]] such as vulnerability caused by the C677T [[methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase]] (MTHFR) variant. Supplementing folic acid during pregnancy reduces the prevalence of NTDs by not exposing this otherwise sub-clinical mutation to aggravating conditions.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Yan|first1=L|last2=Zhao|first2=L|last3=Long|first3=Y|last4=Zou|first4=P|last5=Ji|first5=G|last6=Gu|first6=A|last7=Zhao|first7=P|date=October 3, 2012|title=Association of the Maternal MTHFR C677T Polymorphism with Susceptibility to Neural Tube Defects in Offsprings: Evidence from 25 Case-Control Studies|journal=PLOS ONE|volume=7|issue=10|article-number=e41689|doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0041689|pmid=23056169|pmc=3463537|bibcode=2012PLoSO...741689Y|doi-access=free}} {{open access}}</ref> Other potential causes can include folate [[antimetabolite]]s (such as [[methotrexate]]), [[mycotoxins]] in contaminated corn meal, [[arsenic]], [[hyperthermia]] in early development, and radiation.<ref>{{EMedicine|article|1177162|Neural Tube Defects}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | last1 = Suarez | first1 = L. | last2 = Brender | first2 = J. D. | last3 = Langlois | first3 = P. H. | last4 = Zhan | first4 = F. B. | last5 = Moody | first5 = K. | year = 2007 | title = Pregnant women taking medication for epilepsy have a higher chance of having a child with a neural tube defect. Maternal exposures to hazardous waste sites and industrial facilities and risk of neural tube defects in offspring | journal = Annals of Epidemiology | volume = 17 | issue = 10| pages = 772–77 | pmid = 17689262 | doi = 10.1016/j.annepidem.2007.05.005 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | doi = 10.1111/j.1530-0277.2008.00722.x | last1 = Zhou | first1 = F. C. | last2 = Fang | first2 = Y. | last3 = Goodlett | first3 = C. | year = 2008 | title = Peptidergic Agonists of Activity-Dependent Neurotrophic Factor Protect Against Prenatal Alcohol-Induced Neural Tube Defects and Serotonin Neuron Loss | journal = Alcoholism: Clinical and Experimental Research | volume = 32 | issue = 8| pages = 1361–71 | pmid=18565153 | pmc = 2758042}}</ref> Maternal obesity has also been found to be a risk factor for NTDs.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Huang|first1=Hai-Yan|last2=Chen|first2=Hong-Lin|last3=Feng|first3=Li-Ping|date=March 2017|title=Maternal obesity and the risk of neural tube defects in offspring: A meta-analysis|journal=Obesity Research & Clinical Practice|volume=11|issue=2|pages=188–197|doi=10.1016/j.orcp.2016.04.005|issn=1871-403X|pmid=27155922}}</ref> Studies have shown that both [[Smoking and pregnancy|maternal cigarette smoking]] and maternal exposure to secondhand smoke increased the risk for neural tube defects in offspring.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Wang|first1=M|last2=Wang|first2=ZP|last3=Gong|first3=R|last4=Zhao|first4=ZT|title=Maternal smoking during pregnancy and neural tube defects in offspring: a meta-analysis.|journal=Child's Nervous System|date=January 2014|volume=30|issue=1|pages=83–89|pmid=23760473|doi=10.1007/s00381-013-2194-5|s2cid=40996359}}</ref> A mechanism by which maternal exposure to cigarette smoke could increase NTD risk in offspring is suggested by several studies that show an association between cigarette smoking and elevations of [[homocysteine]] levels.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Suarez |first1=Lucina |last2=Ramadhani |first2=Tunu |last3=Felkner |first3=Marilyn |last4=Canfield |first4=Mark A. |last5=Brender |first5=Jean D. |last6=Romitti |first6=Paul A. |last7=Sun |first7=Lixian |date=2011 |title=Maternal Smoking, Passive Tobacco Smoke, and Neural Tube Defects |journal=Birth Defects Research. Part A, Clinical and Molecular Teratology |volume=91 |issue=1 |pages=29–33 |doi=10.1002/bdra.20743 |issn=1542-0752 |pmc=6034638 |pmid=21254356}}</ref> Cigarette smoke during pregnancy, including secondhand exposure, can increase the risk of neural tube defects.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Meng|first1=Xin|last2=Sun|first2=Yanxin|last3=Duan|first3=Wenhou|last4=Jia|first4=Chongqi|title=Meta-analysis of the association of maternal smoking and passive smoking during pregnancy with neural tube defects|journal=International Journal of Gynecology & Obstetrics|volume=140|issue=1|language=en|pages=18–25|doi=10.1002/ijgo.12334|pmid=28963797|issn=1879-3479|year=2018|s2cid=20885736}}</ref> All of the above may act by interference with some aspect of normal [[folic acid]] metabolism and folate linked methylation related cellular processes as there are multiple genes of this type associated with neural tube defects.<ref name="Genetics of human neural tube defects">{{cite journal | doi = 10.1093/hmg/ddp347 | pmid=19808787 | pmc=2758708 | volume=18 | title=Genetics of human neural tube defects | date=October 2009 | vauthors=Greene ND, Stanier P, Copp AJ | journal=Hum. Mol. Genet. | issue=R2 | pages=R113–29}}</ref>

In addition to MTHFR variants, genetic polymorphisms in [[MTHFD1|methylenetetrahydrofolate dehydrogenase 1]] (MTHFD1) can also influence NTDs risk by increasing the body's dependence on [[choline]]-derived methyl groups to compensate for impaired folate metabolism. One study found that premenopausal women with MTHFD1 variants were significantly more likely to experience organ dysfunction related to choline insufficient and had a heightened risk of NTDs in pregnancy.<ref name="Kenny2025"/>

===Other===
Folic acid supplementation reduces the prevalence of neural tube defects by approximately 70%, indicating that 30% are not folate-dependent and are due to some cause other than alterations of methylation patterns.<ref>{{cite journal | pmid = 1677062 | volume=338 | issue=8760 |doi=10.1016/0140-6736(91)90133-a | title=Prevention of neural tube defects: results of the Medical Research Council Vitamin Study. MRC Vitamin Study Research Group | journal=Lancet | pages=131–37| year=1991 | s2cid=14225812 }}</ref> Multiple other genes related to neural tube defects exist which are candidates for folate insensitive neural tube defects.<ref name="Genetics of human neural tube defects" /> There are also several syndromes such as [[Meckel syndrome]], and [[triploid syndrome]] which are frequently accompanied by neural tube defects that are assumed to be unrelated to folate metabolism<ref>Rose, N, Mennuti, M, Glob. [https://www.glowm.com/section_view/heading/Fetal%20Neural%20Tube%20Defects:%20Diagnosis,%20Management,%20and%20Treatment/item/224 Fetal Neural Tube Defects: Diagnosis, Management, and Treatment] libr. women's med., ({{ISSN|1756-2228}}) 2009; {{doi|10.3843/GLOWM.10224}}</ref>

==Diagnosis==
Tests for neural tube defects include [[Triple test|ultrasound examination]] and measurement of maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein ([[Alpha-fetoprotein|MSAFP]]). Second trimester ultrasound is recommended as the primary screening tool for NTDs, and MSAFP as a secondary screening tool.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal|last1=Wilson|first1=R. Douglas|last2=Audibert|first2=Francois|last3=Brock|first3=Jo-Ann|last4=Campagnolo|first4=Carla|last5=Carroll|first5=June|last6=Cartier|first6=Lola|last7=Chitayat|first7=David|last8=Gagnon|first8=Alain|last9=Johnson|first9=Jo-Ann|title=Prenatal Screening, Diagnosis, and Pregnancy Management of Fetal Neural Tube Defects|journal=Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology Canada|volume=36|issue=10|pages=927–939|doi=10.1016/s1701-2163(15)30444-8|pmid=25375307|date=October 2014|doi-access=free}}{{Retracted|doi=10.1016/j.jogc.2022.06.009|pmid=35934417}}</ref> This is due to increased safety, increased sensitivity and decreased false positive rate of ultrasound as compared to MSAFP.<ref name=":0" /> Amniotic fluid alpha-fetoprotein (AFAFP) and amniotic fluid acetylcholinesterase (AFAChE) tests are also used to confirming if ultrasound screening indicates a positive risk.<ref>{{cite journal | doi = 10.1001/jama.252.11.1438 | last1 = Milunsky | first1 = A | last2 = Alpert | first2 = E. |name-list-style=vanc | year = 1984 | title = Results and benefits of a maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein screening program | journal = JAMA | volume = 252 | issue = 11| pages = 1438–42 | pmid = 6206249 }}</ref> Often, these defects are apparent at birth, but acute defects may not be diagnosed until much later in life. An elevated MSAFP measured at 16–18 weeks gestation is a good predictor of open neural tube defects, however the test has a very high false positive rate, (2% of all women tested in Ontario, Canada between 1993 and 2000 tested positive without having an open neural tube defect, although 5% is the commonly quoted result worldwide) and only a portion of neural tube defects are detected by this screen test (73% in the same Ontario study).<ref>Summer [http://msc.sagepub.com/content/10/3/107.short Maternal Serum Screening in Ontario Using the Triple Marker Test] J Med Screen September 2003 vol. 10 no. 3 107–11.</ref> MSAFP screening combined with routine ultrasonography has the best detection rate although detection by ultrasonography is dependent on operator training and the quality of the equipment.<ref>{{Cite journal | doi=10.1111/j.1471-0528.2008.01700.x| pmid=18410651| pmc=2344123|title = Survey of prenatal screening policies in Europe for structural malformations and chromosome anomalies, and their impact on detection and termination rates for neural tube defects and Down's syndrome| journal=BJOG: An International Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology| volume=115| issue=6| pages=689–696|year = 2008|last1 = Boyd|first1 = PA| last2=Devigan| first2=C.| last3=Khoshnood| first3=B.| last4=Loane| first4=M.| last5=Garne| first5=E.| last6=Dolk| first6=H.}}</ref><ref>Norem et.al [http://journals.lww.com/greenjournal/Abstract/2005/10000/Routine_Ultrasonography_Compared_With_Maternal.14.aspx Routine Ultrasonography Compared With Maternal Serum Alpha-fetoprotein for Neural Tube Defect Screening] Obstetrics & Gynecology: October 2005 Vol 106:4 pp. 747–52</ref>

==Prevention==
Incidence of neural tube defects has been shown to decline through maintenance of adequate folic acid levels prior to and during pregnancy. This is achieved through dietary sources and supplementation of folic acid.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=De Wals |first1=Philippe |last2=Tairou |first2=Fassiatou |last3=Van Allen |first3=Margot I. |last4=Uh |first4=Soo-Hong |last5=Lowry |first5=R. Brian |last6=Sibbald |first6=Barbara |last7=Evans |first7=Jane A. |last8=Van den Hof |first8=Michiel C. |last9=Zimmer |first9=Pamela |last10=Crowley |first10=Marian |last11=Fernandez |first11=Bridget |date=2007-07-12 |title=Reduction in Neural-Tube Defects after Folic Acid Fortification in Canada |journal=New England Journal of Medicine |language=en |volume=357 |issue=2 |pages=135–142 |doi=10.1056/NEJMoa067103 |pmid=17625125 |issn=0028-4793|doi-access=free }}</ref> In 1996, the United States [[Food and Drug Administration]] published regulations requiring the addition of folic acid to enriched breads, cereals, flour and other grain products.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Daly |first1=S |last2=Mills |first2=JL |last3=Molloy |first3=AM |last4=Conley |first4=M |last5=Lee |first5=YJ |last6=Kirke |first6=PN |last7=Weir |first7=DG |last8=Scott |first8=JM |name-list-style=vanc |year=1997 |title=Minimum effective dose of folic acid for food fortification to prevent neural-tube defects |journal=Lancet |volume=350 |issue=9092 |pages=1666–69 |doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(97)07247-4 |pmid=9400511 |s2cid=39708487}}</ref> Similar regulations made it mandatory to fortify selected grain products with folic acid in Canada by 1998.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ray |first=Joel G. |date=2004-06-01 |title=Folic Acid Food Fortification in Canada |journal=Nutrition Reviews |language=en |volume=62 |issue=6 |pages=35–39 |doi=10.1111/j.1753-4887.2004.tb00072.x | doi-access=free |pmid=15298446 }}</ref> During the first four weeks of pregnancy (when most women do not even realize that they are pregnant), adequate folate intake is essential for proper operation of the [[neurulation]] process. Therefore, any individuals who could become pregnant are advised to eat foods fortified with folic acid or take supplements in addition to eating folate-rich foods to reduce the risks of serious birth defects.<ref name="Greene, N. D. E. 20092">{{cite journal |last1=Greene |first1=ND |last2=Stanier |first2=P |last3=Copp |first3=AJ |year=2009 |title=Genetics of human neural tube defects |journal=Human Molecular Genetics |volume=18 |issue=R2 |pages=R113–29 |doi=10.1093/hmg/ddp347 |pmc=2758708 |pmid=19808787}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Milunsky |first1=A |last2=Jick |first2=H |last3=Jick |first3=SS |last4=Bruell |first4=CL |last5=MacLaughlin |first5=DS |last6=Rothman |first6=KJ |last7=Willett |first7=W |name-list-style=vanc |year=1989 |title=Multivitamin/folic acid supplementation in early pregnancy reduces the prevalence of neural tube defects |journal=Journal of the American Medical Association |volume=262 |issue=20 |pages=2847–52 |doi=10.1001/jama.262.20.2847 |pmid=2478730}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Goh |first1=YI |last2=Koren |first2=G |year=2008 |title=Folic acid in pregnancy and fetal outcomes |journal=J. Obstet. Gynaecol. |volume=28 |issue=1 |pages=3–13 |doi=10.1080/01443610701814195 |pmid=18259891 |s2cid=28654601}}</ref> In Canada, mandatory fortification of selected foods with folic acid had been shown to reduce the incidence of neural tube defects by 46% compared to incidence prior to mandatory fortification.<ref>{{cite journal |author=De Wals P |author2=Tairou F |author3=Van Allen MI |last4=Uh |first4=Soo-Hong |last5=Lowry |first5=R. Brian |last6=Sibbald |first6=Barbara |last7=Evans |first7=Jane A. |last8=Van Den Hof |first8=Michiel C. |last9=Zimmer |first9=Pamela |display-authors=3 |name-list-style=vanc |year=2007 |title=Reduction in neural-tube defects after folic acid fortification in Canada |journal=N Engl J Med |volume=357 |issue=2 |pages=135–42 |doi=10.1056/NEJMoa067103 |pmid=17625125|doi-access=free }}</ref> However, relying on eating a folate-rich diet alone is not recommended for preventing neural tube defects when trying to conceive because a regular diet usually does not contain enough folate to reach pregnancy requirements.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Naithani |first1=Manisha |last2=Saxena |first2=Vartika |last3=Mirza |first3=Anissa Atif |last4=Kumari |first4=Ranjeeta |last5=Sharma |first5=Kapil |last6=Bharadwaj |first6=Jyoti |date=2016 |title=Assessment of Folic Acid Supplementation in Pregnant Women by Estimation of Serum Levels of Tetrahydrofolic Acid, Dihydrofolate Reductase, and Homocysteine |journal=Scientifica |language=en |volume=2016 |pages=1–5 |doi=10.1155/2016/1520685 |issn=2090-908X |pmc=4811260 |pmid=27064332|doi-access=free }}</ref><ref name=":12">{{Cite web |last=Canada |first=Health |date=2009-04-28 |title=Prenatal Nutrition Guidelines for Health Professionals - Folate Contributes to a Healthy Pregnancy |url=https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/publications/food-nutrition/prenatal-nutrition-guidelines-health-professionals-folate-contributes-healthy-pregnancy-2009.html |access-date=2021-12-01 |website=www.canada.ca}}</ref> All individuals who have the ability to become pregnant are advised to get 400 micrograms of folic acid daily.<ref name=":22">{{Cite web |last=Canada |first=Public Health Agency of |date=2018-01-05 |title=Folic acid and neural tube defects |url=https://www.canada.ca/en/public-health/services/pregnancy/folic-acid.html |access-date=2022-02-27 |website=www.canada.ca}}</ref><ref name=":02">{{Cite journal |last1=Wilson |first1=R. Douglas |last2=Genetics Committee |last3=Wilson |first3=R. Douglas |last4=Audibert |first4=François |last5=Brock |first5=Jo-Ann |last6=Carroll |first6=June |last7=Cartier |first7=Lola |last8=Gagnon |first8=Alain |last9=Johnson |first9=Jo-Ann |last10=Langlois |first10=Sylvie |last11=Murphy-Kaulbeck |first11=Lynn |date=June 2015 |title=Pre-conception Folic Acid and Multivitamin Supplementation for the Primary and Secondary Prevention of Neural Tube Defects and Other Folic Acid-Sensitive Congenital Anomalies |journal=Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology Canada |volume=37 |issue=6 |pages=534–552 |doi=10.1016/s1701-2163(15)30230-9 |issn=1701-2163 |pmid=26334606|doi-access=free }}</ref> This daily 400&nbsp;mcg dose of folic acid can be found in most multivitamins advertised as for women.<ref name="FDA_Folic_Rec2">{{cite journal |author=Centers for Disease Control |date=11 September 1992 |title=Recommendations for the Use of Folic Acid to Reduce the Number of Cases of Spina Bifida and Other Neural Tube Defects |url=https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/00019479.htm |journal=Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report |volume=41 |issue=RR-14 |page=001}}</ref> Higher doses can be found in pre-natal multivitamins but those doses may not be necessary for everyone.<ref>{{Cite web |last=CDC |date=2021-04-19 |title=Folic Acid |url=https://www.cdc.gov/ncbddd/folicacid/about.html |access-date=2022-02-27 |website=Centers for Disease Control and Prevention |language=en-us}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Canada |first=Health |date=2009-04-28 |title=Prenatal Nutrition Guidelines for Health Professionals - Folate Contributes to a Healthy Pregnancy |url=https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/publications/food-nutrition/prenatal-nutrition-guidelines-health-professionals-folate-contributes-healthy-pregnancy-2009.html |access-date=2022-02-27 |website=www.canada.ca}}</ref> Individuals who have previously given birth to a child with a neural tube defect and are trying to conceive again may benefit from a supplement containing 4.0&nbsp;mg daily, following advice provided by their doctor.<ref name="FDA_Folic_Rec2" />  In Canada, guidelines on folic acid intake when trying to conceive is based on a risk assessment of how likely they are to experience a neural tube defect during pregnancy. Risk is divided into high, moderate, and low risk categories.<ref name=":02" /> High risk would include those that had a past experience with neural tube defects, either themselves or during another pregnancy.<ref name=":02" /> Medium risk individuals are those with certain conditions that put them at higher risk for experiencing a neural tube defect. These include having a first or second degree relative or partner with a history of neural tube defects, having a gastrointestinal condition that affects normal absorption patterns, advanced [[kidney disease]], [[kidney dialysis]], alcohol over-use, or had another pregnancy resulting in a congenital abnormality that was folate sensitive. Medium risk individuals would also include those taking medications that can interfere with folate absorption such as [[anticonvulsant]]s, [[metformin]], [[sulfasalazine]], [[triamterene]], and [[trimethoprim]].<ref name=":02" /> Low risk would include everyone else that do not fall into either medium or high risk categories. Recommendations on when to start folic acid supplementation for all individuals looking to become pregnant is at least three months preconception.<ref name=":12" /><ref name=":02" /> If an individual is in the high risk category, the recommended dose is 4–5&nbsp;mg of folic acid daily until 12 weeks gestation and then decrease to 0.4–1&nbsp;mg until 4–6 weeks postpartum or for however long breastfeeding lasts.<ref name=":02" /> If an individual is in the medium risk category, the recommended dose is 1&nbsp;mg of folic acid daily until 12 weeks gestation and then they can either continue at 1&nbsp;mg or decrease to 0.4&nbsp;mg daily until 4–6 weeks postpartum or however long breastfeeding lasts.<ref name=":02" /> If the pregnancy is low risk to develop a neural tube defect then the recommendation for that individual is 0.4&nbsp;mg daily until 4–6 weeks postpartum or however long breastfeeding lasts.<ref name=":02" /> All dose recommendations and risk assessment should be done with the advice of a qualified health care provider.<ref name=":22" />

==Treatment==
As of 2008, treatments of NTDs depends on the severity of the complication. No treatment is available for anencephaly and infants usually do not survive more than a few hours. Aggressive surgical management has improved survival and the functions of infants with spina bifida, meningoceles and mild myelomeningoceles.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Copp |first1=Andrew J. |last2=Stanier |first2=Philip |last3=Greene |first3=Nicholas D. E. |date=2013 |title=Neural tube defects – recent advances, unsolved questions and controversies |journal=Lancet Neurology |volume=12 |issue=8 |pages=799–810 |doi=10.1016/S1474-4422(13)70110-8 |issn=1474-4422 |pmc=4023229 |pmid=23790957}}</ref> The success of surgery often depends on the amount of brain tissue involved in the encephalocele. The goal of treatment for NTDs is to allow the individual to achieve the highest level of function, and independence. Fetal surgery in utero before 26 weeks gestation has been performed with some hope that there is benefit to the outcome including a reduction in [[Arnold–Chiari malformation]] and thereby decreases the need for a [[ventriculoperitoneal shunt]] but the procedure is very high risk for both mother and baby and is considered extremely invasive with questions that the positive outcomes may be due to [[ascertainment bias]] and not true benefit. Further, this surgery is not a cure for all problems associated with a neural tube defect. Other areas of research include tissue engineering and stem cell therapy but this research has not been used in humans.<ref>{{Cite journal | doi=10.1016/j.bpobgyn.2007.07.004| pmid=17714997| pmc=2293328| title=Fetal surgery for neural tube defects| journal=Best Practice & Research Clinical Obstetrics & Gynaecology| volume=22| issue=1| pages=175–188| year=2008| last1=Sutton| first1=Leslie N.}}</ref>

==Epidemiology==
[[File:Neural tube defects world map-Deaths per million persons-WHO2012.svg|thumb|upright=1.3|Deaths from neural tube defects per million persons in 2012 {{Div col|small=yes|colwidth=10em}}{{legend|#ffff20|0–0}}{{legend|#ffd820|1–1}}{{legend|#ffc020|2–3}}{{legend|#ffa020|4–6}}{{legend|#ff9a20|7–10}}{{legend|#f08015|11–15}}{{legend|#e06815|16–20}}{{legend|#d85010|21–28}}{{legend|#d02010|29–69}}{{div col end}}]]

Neural tube defects resulted in 71,000 deaths globally in 2010.<ref name=Loz2012>{{cite journal|last=Lozano|first=R|title=Global and regional mortality from 235 causes of death for 20 age groups in 1990 and 2010: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2010.|journal=Lancet|date=Dec 15, 2012|volume=380|issue=9859|pages=2095–128|pmid=23245604|doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(12)61728-0|pmc=10790329|hdl=10536/DRO/DU:30050819|s2cid=1541253|url=https://zenodo.org/record/2557786|hdl-access=free}}</ref> It is unclear how common the condition is in low income countries.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Zaganjor|first1=Ibrahim|last2=Sekkarie|first2=Ahlia|last3=Tsang|first3=Becky L.|last4=Williams|first4=Jennifer|last5=Razzaghi|first5=Hilda|last6=Mulinare|first6=Joseph|last7=Sniezek|first7=Joseph E.|last8=Cannon|first8=Michael J.|last9=Rosenthal|first9=Jorge|date=2016-04-11|title=Describing the Prevalence of Neural Tube Defects Worldwide: A Systematic Literature Review|journal=PLOS ONE|volume=11|issue=4|article-number=e0151586|doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0151586|issn=1932-6203|pmc=4827875|pmid=27064786|bibcode=2016PLoSO..1151586Z|doi-access=free}}</ref>

Prevalence rates of NTDs at birth used to be a reliable measure for the actual number of children affected by the diseases.<ref name=":4">{{Cite journal|last1=Frey|first1=Lauren|last2=Hauser|first2=W. Allen|date=2003|title=Epidemiology of Neural Tube Defects|journal=Epilepsia|language=en|volume=44|issue=s3|pages=4–13|doi=10.1046/j.1528-1157.44.s3.2.x|pmid=12790881|issn=1528-1167|doi-access=free}}</ref> However, due to advances in technology and the ability to diagnose prenatally, the rates at birth are no longer reliable.<ref name=":4" /> Measuring the number of cases at birth may be the most practical way, but the most accurate way would be to include stillbirths and live-births.<ref name=":4" /> Most studies that calculate prevalence rates only include data from live births and stillborn children and normally exclude the data from abortions and miscarriages.<ref name=":4" /> Abortions are a huge contributing factor to the prevalence rates; one study found that in 1986 only a quarter of the pregnancies with an identified NTD were aborted, but that number had already doubled by 1999.<ref name=":4" /> Through this data, it is clear that excluding data from abortions could greatly affect the prevalence rates. This could also possibly explain why prevalence rates have appeared to drop. If abortions are not being included in the data but half of the identified cases are being aborted, the data could show that prevalence rates are dropping when they actually are not. However, it is unclear how much of an impact these could have on prevalence rates due to the fact that abortion rates and advances in technology vary greatly by country.<ref name=":4" />

There are many maternal factors that also play a role in prevalence rates of NTDs.<ref name=":4" /> These factors include things like maternal age and obesity all the way to things like socioeconomic status along with many others.<ref name=":4" /> Maternal age has not been shown to have a huge impact on prevalence rates, but when there has been a relationship identified, older mothers along with very young mothers are at an increased risk.<ref name=":4" /> While maternal age may not have a huge impact, mothers that have a body mass index greater than 29 double the risk of their child having an NTD.<ref name=":4" /> Studies have also shown that mothers with three or more previous children show moderate risk for their next child having an NTD.<ref name=":4" />
